From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 01:12:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: add a stateless H.264 encoder

The Allwinner video engine has an H.264 encoder next to its decoders:
an input stage ("ISP", registers 0xa00) that reads the NV12 picture and
an AVC encoder block (0xb00) with a bit writer for the headers, a
sub-pixel reference plane and a macroblock information buffer. Mainline
cedrus never touched it.

Add the encoder as a second video device ("cedrus-enc") on the same M2M
device as the decoder, so that its jobs and the decoding jobs are
serialised on the engine, and drive it with the stateless H.264 encode
core (media: h264: Add stateless encode core and its follow-ups):
userspace provides the SPS, the PPS and the encode parameters of each
picture through requests, the core adapts them to the hardware
(state_constrain: 8-bit 4:2:0 frames, one reference, CAVLC, no 8x8
transform), writes the AUD, SPS, PPS and the slice header, tracks the
reference pictures and picks the QP (direct QP, constant quality or
constant bitrate), and the engine appends the slice data. The headers go
through the engine's bit writer: the core's bits operations are
implemented on it, raw bits (start codes, NAL unit headers) with the
emulation prevention off and the rest with it on, switched at byte
boundaries only, as the vendor library does. The engine's reconstruction
and sub-pixel buffers are the core's reconstruction buffers, with the
sizes of the V3s encoder.

The register programming is that of Paul Kocialkowski's V3s encoder
(cedrus/h264-encoding branch, 2023), with variant flags for the 0x12011
encoder generation of the H6 and H616 as the vendor library drives it
(recorded on the H616 by the Hardened-BliKVM lab, 2026-10): the encoder
block address registers take addresses in units of 256 bytes, the ISP
takes the visible size in units of 8 pixels and a second stride copy,
and the vendor's motion estimation parameters (dynamic ME with its
thresholds, PARA1 bit 27, ME_PARA 0x11000, no chroma QP offset).
Frames start at 16x16; P pictures of frames narrower than 13 macroblocks
never finish on the H616, they are encoded as I pictures. Pictures wider
than 2048 need the external deblocking line buffer and are refused for
now, as is CABAC until the "fix mode num" field is understood.

The engine stops at the end of the coded buffer and raises a stall,
which fails the picture; it never writes past the buffer. The DMA
addresses are the IOMMU's, all masters stay translated.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
